    "summary": "Acoustic gems shine, Cosmic Charlie dazzles; electric set uneven",
    "blurb": "Rare acoustic numbers like How Long Blues, Deep Elem and Cumberland Blues dominate a relaxed first set. The second half delivers highlights such as Cosmic Charlie, a funky China Cat → I Know You Rider transition, and a solid closing with Uncle John's Band despite some electric mix issues.",
    "review": "July 14 1970 at the Euphoria Ballroom captures the Dead in a uniquely balanced mood. The first set is an acoustic treasure trove – the band rolls out seldom‑heard gems like How Long Blues, Deep Elem Blues and Cumberland Blues before launching into a bright New Speedway Boogie where Jerry’s classic tone glows. The second set picks up the electric charge with a standout Cosmic Charlie that really “smokes,” while the China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue erupts in funky, high‑in‑the‑mix percussion from Mickey and Bill. Though the mix on the electric side suffers occasional buzzing and a few cuts (Casey Jones, Easy Wind), the overall performance remains compelling, capped by an uplifting Uncle John's Band encore.\n\nTwo soundboard sources give us slightly different pictures: the “unknown” SBD (gd1970-07-14.sbd.unknown.96628.sbeok.shnf) offers crisp, warm 1970‑era fidelity with only minor glitches, making it the go‑to listen for the full show’s flow. The Cotsman SBD adds a raw edge and highlights the guest‑jam vibe on the acoustic side but is marred by buzzing on the electric numbers. For most Deadheads looking to soak in this rare acoustic‑electric hybrid, the first recording provides the cleanest window into what made this night special.",
